---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import { Card, Table, Alert } from "webcoreui/astro";

const tableHeadings = [
    "Spell Cast",
    "Cards Seen",
    "Chance to find at least 1 of a 4-of",
    "Chance to find at least 1 of a 3-of",
];
const tableData = [
    ["<strong>Frantic Search</strong>", "2", "15.1%", "11.4%"],
    ["<strong>Attunement</strong> (1 activation)", "3", "22.2%", "16.9%"],
    ["<strong>Fact or Fiction</strong>", "5", "35.3%", "27.3%"],
];
---

<Layout
    title="Mulligan Guide - Replenish Guide | Premodern MTG"
    image="/src/assets/banners/search.webp"
    description="When to keep or mulligan your opening hand with the Replenish deck in MTG Premodern."
>
    <PageHeader
        title="Mulligan Guide"
        description="Knowing when to keep and when to throw back your opening hand is one of the most important skills with Replenish."
        imageSrc="/src/assets/banners/search.webp"
        imageAlt="Frantic Search"
        imageCaption="Frantic Search by Jeff Miracola"
        isCard={true}
    />

    <Card title="Mulligan Philosophy" titleTag="h2">
        <p>
            With <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Parallax Tide"
                >Parallax Tide</a
            > banned, the deck went from having 8 hand-fixers to just 4. You can't
            rely on drawing out of a bad hand anymore. <strong
                >You need to mulligan aggressively in Game 1 in the dark.</strong
            > Don't keep slow hands hoping to topdeck exactly what you need.
        </p>

        <Alert theme="info">
            <strong>The 5-Point Checklist (Game 1, in the blind)</strong><br />
            See how many of these boxes your opening hand ticks:<br /><br />
            1. <strong>Interaction</strong> (Swords, Seal, Leak)<br />
            2. <strong>A 3-mana blue Spell</strong> (Attunement, Intuition, Frantic
            Search)<br />
            3. <strong>A 4+ mana enchantment</strong> (Opal, Wave, Decree)<br />
            4. <strong>Replenish</strong><br />
            5. <strong>4+ mana</strong> (Lands/Tomb)<br /><br />
            <strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> Keep if you have <strong
                >3 or 4</strong
            > of these. Anything with 2 or fewer is probably a mulligan.
        </Alert>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Hands You Should Keep" titleTag="h2">
        <p>
            Assuming you have the lands to cast your spells, these are solid
            starting hands in the dark:
        </p>
        <ul style="padding-left: 1.25rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <li class="mb-sm">
                <strong>Engine + Lands:</strong>
                <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Attunement"
                    >Attunement</a
                > + lands. This is your best possible start.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-sm">
                <strong>Combo + Lands:</strong>
                <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Opalescence"
                    >Opalescence</a
                > + <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
                > + lands.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-sm">
                <strong>Tutor + Piece:</strong>
                <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Intuition"
                    >Intuition</a
                > + any combo piece (Opal, Wave, Replenish).
            </li>
            <li class="mb-sm">
                <strong>Heavy Control:</strong>
                <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Mana Leak"
                    >Mana Leak</a
                > + at least two other interactive spells (Swords, Seal).
            </li>
            <li class="mb-sm">
                <strong>Setup:</strong>
                <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Replenish"
                    >Replenish</a
                > + <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
                > + an expensive permanent to discard (Opal, Wave, Decree).
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Early Board Control:</strong>
                <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Parallax Wave"
                    >Parallax Wave</a
                > + <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                >.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-10);">
            Ancient Tomb
        </h3>
        <p class="mb-0">
            <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Ancient Tomb"
                >Ancient Tomb</a
            > speeds up your game plan entirely. Hands like <strong
                >Tomb + Intuition + (Swords/Leak/Frantic)</strong
            > are usually instant keeps.
        </p>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Hands You Should Mulligan" titleTag="h2">
        <p>Don't fall into the trap of keeping these hands in the dark:</p>
        <ul class="pl-md mb-0">
            <li class="mb-sm">
                <strong>The "Do Nothing" Hand:</strong> 4 Lands + Opal + Decree +
                Seal. You have to topdeck perfectly for several turns to actually
                do anything.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-sm">
                <strong>Flooded:</strong> 5 Lands + 2 interaction spells. You will
                likely draw combo pieces with no way to discard them.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Dead Weight:</strong>
                <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Replenish"
                    >Replenish</a
                > without an enabler (Attunement, Frantic, or Intuition) is a dead
                card.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </Card>

    <Card title="The Math" titleTag="h2">
        <p>
            Assuming you are on the play and cast a draw spell on Turn 3 (51
            cards left in deck), here is the chance to find a specific card you
            need (like Replenish):
        </p>
        <div
            style="margin-bottom: 2rem; margin-top: 1rem; overflow-x: auto; width: 100%; max-width: 100%;"
        >
            <Table
                headings={tableHeadings}
                data={tableData}
                hover={true}
                striped="row"
            />
        </div>
        <p class="mb-0">
            <strong>The Frantic Search Paradox:</strong> As the table shows, a single Frantic Search gives you just a 15% chance to find one specific 4-of. This is why keeping a hand where you desperately need Frantic Search to find exactly Replenish is a huge gamble. <br /><br />
            However, if your hand is flexible and you have 15 different "outs" that make the hand work (e.g. any Replenish, Wave, Attunement, or Intuition), your odds jump to ~75% across your first three turns. Frantic Search shines as a discard outlet when you already have Replenish, or when you have many outs, but it is a weak engine on its own.
        </p>
    </Card>


</Layout>

<style>
    .debate-grid {
        display: grid;
        grid-template-columns: 1fr;
        gap: 1.5rem;
        margin-top: 1.5rem;
    }

    @media (min-width: 768px) {
        .debate-grid {
            grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
        }
    }

    .debate-box {
        padding: 1.25rem;
        border-radius: var(--w-md-radius);
        border-left-width: 4px;
        border-left-style: solid;
    }

    .debate-title {
        display: block;
        margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
        font-size: 1.1rem;
    }

    .keep-box {
        background-color: rgba(46, 204, 113, 0.08);
        border-left-color: var(--w-color-success);
    }

    .keep-title {
        color: var(--w-color-success);
    }

    .mulligan-box {
        background-color: rgba(231, 76, 60, 0.08);
        border-left-color: var(--w-color-danger);
    }

    .mulligan-title {
        color: var(--w-color-danger);
    }
</style>
